## Noncommercial Purposes

Any noncommercial purpose is a permitted purpose.

A commercial purpose is any purpose meant to earn money or
another commercial advantage.  For example: selling the
software, a copy of it, or anything made with it or taken
from it; charging for access to any of them; showing
advertising in the software or on a page, app or service
that offers it; running paid servers, services or tools
built on it; and using it in a commercial product or
service, or in the work of a business.  None of these is a
permitted purpose.

Teaching with the software, in a school, a course, or a
class, paid or free, is use for a permitted purpose, as long
as nobody is charged for the software itself or for access
to it.

## Personal Uses

Personal use for research, experiment, and testing for
the benefit of public knowledge, personal study, private
entertainment, hobby projects, amateur pursuits, or religious
observance, without any anticipated commercial application,
is use for a permitted purpose.

## Noncommercial Organizations

Use by any charitable organization, educational institution,
public research organization, public safety or health
organization, environmental protection organization,
or government institution is use for a permitted purpose
regardless of the source of funding or obligations resulting
from the funding.

## Videos and Streams

Recording, streaming, or publishing videos or pictures of
yourself using the software is use for a permitted purpose,
even on a channel or page that earns money, as long as you
do not sell, rent, or charge for the software itself or for
access to it.

## Additional Conditions

Every license these terms give you is also given on the
following conditions.  A use that breaks one of them is not
use for a permitted purpose, whatever any other section of
these terms says.

1. No online multiplayer.  You may not use the software, or
anything made with it or taken from it, to make, run, or
help run a multiplayer game, server, or service that people
join over a network.  Using the software on your own
computers, for yourself, is fine.

2. A changed copy is not the original.  If you distribute
the software with changes, or a new work based on it, you
must give it a name and a logo of its own (never the name
iStrongEngine or its logo), say plainly in it that it is a
changed, unofficial copy, and never say or suggest that it
comes from the licensor or is approved by the licensor.
You may say truthfully that it is based on iStrongEngine.

3. No passing off.  You may not present the software, any
part of it, or any game or game content remade with it, as
your own original creation, and you may not claim to own
another game or its content because you remade it with the
software.

